WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.190
On January 1st, 2019, Jair Bolsonaro, one
of the most controversial politicians in international

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:18.920
news, became the president of Brazil.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:22.630
Bolsonaro came to power with a promise: to
change Brazil, the fifth largest country in

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:28.039
the world, a giant that has more than 200
million people, from top to bottom in an attempt

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:31.420
to turn it into a political and economic power.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:37.310
However folks, Bolsonaro’s first 100 days
into his term we can find headlines like these:

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:42.460
Jair Bolsonaro, Brazil’s apprentice president.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:47.180
Unless he stops provoking and learns to govern,
his tenure could be short.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.180
The Economist)

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.770
The Christian Coalition That Helped Elect
Bolsonaro Has Started to Crumble.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:53.770
FP)

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:57.030
Bolsanaro’s Popularity Sinks After a Rocky
100 Days in Brazil.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:58.030
New York Times)

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:02.400
Since becoming president, Bolsonaro has continued
with his incendiary and controversial campaign

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.080
speech, remains very active in social networks
and there’s no day in which he doesn’t

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.170
attack other parties and politicians.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:14.080
Which is strange considering he himself has
been in politics 30 years and has three children

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.390
who are also politicians.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:19.580
“My God, what have I done to deserve this?

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:20.580
Nothing but trouble!"

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:21.770
Jair Bolsonaro.)

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:25.450
Now, how can you use controversy to govern
a country?

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:29.220
How can you secure power without seeking pacts
or political support?

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.950
And the most important question of all: What
exactly can we expect from Jair Bolsonaro?

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:52.950
Listen up.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:56.810
October 28th, 2018, was a historic day for
Brazil.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:01.090
In the second round, after a very heated campaign
that caught global attention and in which,

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:06.440
the current president was even stabbed, Jair
Bolsonaro finally won majority support from

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:07.440
Brazilian voters.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.160
Specifically 55.13% of the votes.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:14.620
His victory, dethroned the Workers' Party
– Lula da Silva’s party – a party that

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:20.010
had completely dominated Brazilian politics
for 16 years.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:26.900
Bolsonaro is a character known for his harsh
speech, for his support of the military dictatorship

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:32.190
and for his controversial comments – in
many cases of a homophobic, sexist and even

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:35.340
racist nature.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.910
He promised to end the extensive corruption
that has been ravaging Brazil: to fight relentlessly

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:43.580
against the violence that has turned Brazil
into one of the most dangerous countries in

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:48.130
the world; 50 to 65 thousand people are murdered
there every year.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:53.599
And, of course, to dismantle the gigantic
political framework that is currently crippling

00:02:53.599 --> 00:02:55.920
Brazil’s society and economy.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:01.290
It was precisely his economic promises that
generated the most excitement during his campaign,

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:05.239
causing many Liberals to side with Bolsonaro.

00:03:05.239 --> 00:03:09.849
In turn Bolsonaro delegated the economic section
to a group of Chicago Boys led by the Minister

00:03:09.849 --> 00:03:11.850
of Economy, Paulo Guedes.

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:17.430
And it makes sense to overhaul the economy
because Brazil can’t keep running like it

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:18.430
has been.

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:22.080
But hold on just a moment, Although Bolsonaro
won the presidential elections, he doesn’t

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.880
have a legislative majority.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:29.180
His party only got 55 of the 513 seats that
make up the Chamber of Deputies.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:32.590
And this is important, because if you don’t
have the majority in the legislature, and

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:36.680
you don’t seek political support from other
elected parties, how are you going to get

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:40.090
any laws approved?

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:46.210
Brazil’s Congress and Senate are very fragmented,
which is why previous Brazilian presidents

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:49.520
have traditionally ruled by forming pacts
with other groups.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:52.160
This is known as "Coalition Presidentialism."

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:57.930
However, Bolsonaro simply doesn’t work that
way.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:58.930
Of course...

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:05.819
This isn’t the new Brazilian president’s
only handicap.

00:04:05.819 --> 00:04:08.480
Listen up.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.769
(EASY TO SAY, HARD TO DO?)

00:04:13.769 --> 00:04:18.079
Brazil is chaotic, it’s by far one of the
most bureaucratic and complicated countries

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:19.239
in the world...

00:04:19.239 --> 00:04:24.219
And it’s that way for everyone: ordinary
people, companies, and even for politicians.

00:04:24.219 --> 00:04:27.490
Do you want an example of what bureaucracy
means in Brazil?

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:28.490
Well, check this out.

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:33.729
It is estimated that a medium-sized company,
even a small company spends about 2,000 hours

00:04:33.729 --> 00:04:35.139
a year declaring taxes in Brazil.

00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:36.139
That has to be a world record.

00:04:36.139 --> 00:04:40.740
This may help explain why Brazil scores lower
than even Argentina in the economic freedom

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:45.130
index and is considered to be one of the seven
countries in the world where taxes are most

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:48.529
cumbersome to pay, according to the World
Bank's Doing Business index.

00:04:48.529 --> 00:04:52.520
Check this out:

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:59.620
And, on top of that, taxes aren’t even low,
in fact, quite the opposite.

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:02.270
In short, it’s a crazy system.

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:12.330
The point is that it feels like Bolsonaro’s
lack of experience is taking its toll.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:19.220
It may be surprising to hear Bolsonaro described
as inexperienced when he’s been in politics

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:23.169
for more than 30 years, and almost all that
time as a federal deputy.

00:05:23.169 --> 00:05:27.580
However, this time was served in what Brazilian
politics calls ‘low clergy’.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:30.089
That is, politicians with minimal power.

00:05:30.089 --> 00:05:34.080
Characters who are in politics but who have
neither the ability to influence nor take

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.659
part in actual decision-making.

00:05:35.659 --> 00:05:41.720
Well, the fact is that since he was appointed
president, Bolsonaro hasn’t had a clear

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:46.860
agenda, his first months in office have been
a sequence of meaningless fights, controversial

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:48.740
statements, sudden changes of opinion, etcetera,
etcetera.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:55.159
Along the way, two ministers have already
been fired, all in just three months.

00:05:55.159 --> 00:05:59.349
So apart from attacking the press, which happens
all the time, we don’t really know what

00:05:59.349 --> 00:06:01.349
he’ll be up to from one day to the next.

00:06:01.349 --> 00:06:05.210
And, of course, some important ideas have
been discussed, such as commemorating the

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:07.839
1964 coup that led to the dictatorship.

00:06:07.839 --> 00:06:12.909
As if Brazil had no more pressing matters
than opening these debates.

00:06:12.909 --> 00:06:16.009
But that's not all.

00:06:16.009 --> 00:06:19.199
Bolsonaro himself, who came to power as a
champion in the fight against corruption,

00:06:19.199 --> 00:06:21.999
has seen its shadow loom very close to him.

00:06:21.999 --> 00:06:26.169
One of his sons, senator Flavio Bolsonaro,
received 48 “suspicious” deposits worth

00:06:26.169 --> 00:06:31.599
$30,000 in his bank account according to reports
obtained and broadcast by the Jornal Nacional

00:06:31.599 --> 00:06:32.599
newscast.

00:06:32.599 --> 00:06:35.580
Among other scandals, there have been reports
of the diversion of public electoral funds

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:42.249
by Bolsonaro’s Social Liberal Party to “fake
candidates” in the last elections.

00:06:42.249 --> 00:06:46.529
These accusations led to the dismissal of
the General Secretary of the Presidency Gustavo

00:06:46.529 --> 00:06:51.520
Bebianno, who was also none other than the
coordinator of Bolsonaro's own campaign.

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:54.199
That’s a great start.

00:06:54.199 --> 00:06:57.849
And it helps to explain how, in just three
months the president's approval rating among

00:06:57.849 --> 00:07:03.300
Brazilians dropped 16 points and that the
confidence index has gone from 62% in January

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:04.939
to 49% in March.

00:07:04.939 --> 00:07:08.779
Look, we're talking about the worst record
of a Brazilian president at the beginning

00:07:08.779 --> 00:07:10.349
of his term since 1990.

00:07:10.349 --> 00:07:16.629
But, folks, that’s just the start of his
problems.

00:07:16.629 --> 00:07:22.929
(MY ENEMY’S ENEMY IS MY FRIEND)

00:07:22.929 --> 00:07:27.160
An election campaign is always different from
governing a country.

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:31.240
For example, in a campaign you can promise
gold and myrrh and create alliances between

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:35.189
parties and politicians whose only affinity
is their rejection of a different political

00:07:35.189 --> 00:07:36.889
option.

00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:40.189
That’s exactly what happened in Brazil.

00:07:40.189 --> 00:07:43.169
Bolsonaro managed to bring together all the
different groups that were fed up with the

00:07:43.169 --> 00:07:44.500
Workers' Party’s policies.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:48.919
However, there’s one problem: once you reach
power and have to govern, that’s when the

00:07:48.919 --> 00:07:51.139
differences begin to show.

00:07:51.139 --> 00:07:54.689
This is what seems to be happening in Brazil
right now.

00:07:54.689 --> 00:08:00.539
You see, the support system that led Bolsonaro
to the Palácio da Alvorada - the Brazilian

00:08:00.539 --> 00:08:04.849
White House – includes a bit of everything:
anti-Marxists, evangelists, liberals, traditionalists,

00:08:04.849 --> 00:08:06.800
military, conservatives....

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:09.710
And of course, sometimes the differences matter.

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:14.999
In a nutshell, we could say that there are
two main currents within Bolsonaro’s government:

00:08:14.999 --> 00:08:20.699
pragmatists and ideologists.

00:08:20.699 --> 00:08:24.259
Among the the pragmatists, who are those groups
willing to negotiate with other political

00:08:24.259 --> 00:08:26.169
forces, are the military and the liberals.

00:08:26.169 --> 00:08:30.360
For example, the liberal group, led by the
powerful Economy Minister, Pablo Guedes, defended

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:34.800
much more aggressive reforms during the campaign
than those it defends now that they’re in

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:39.570
power, and that’s because they need support
to get those reforms through.

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:45.490
A very clear example is the pension reform.

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:48.880
During the campaign Pablo Guedes defended
reforming the entire system, using the Chilean

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:50.000
model as an example.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.850
In short, promoting a capitalization model.

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:58.620
Now, however, he’s promoting significant
reforms but they are nowhere near as ambitious:

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:03.140
A minimum retirement age, a capped pension
and eligibility based on a required number

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:04.670
of years in the workforce.

00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:11.300
The government will still save a lot of money,
but basically the system structure will remain

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:12.300
the same.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:14.870
And of course, in order to approve even a
moderate pension system reform, they need

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:18.520
three fifths of the congressmen and senators
to vote in favor.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:20.140
So... it’s time to negotiate.

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:23.890
The second current that runs through Bolsonaro’s
government, is the ideological one.

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:26.470
And this group includes the President's own
children.

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:30.490
As I mentioned, the entire family is involved
in politics.

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:33.660
“I’ve never seen anything like it.

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:37.950
I don’t remember any other government in
which the family had so much power as this

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:38.950
one.

00:09:38.950 --> 00:09:39.950
Family is always a problem.

00:09:39.950 --> 00:09:43.300
The president needs to keep some distance
from his family and friends.”

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:46.830
Fernando Henrique Cardoso, former president
of Brazil)

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:51.400
The fact is that the ideologists are the least
willing to negotiate or seek consensus.

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:56.270
It’s piloted by this man, Olavo de Carvalho,
Bolsonaro’s Steve Bannon.

00:09:56.270 --> 00:10:00.500
He’s a self-taught writer and philosopher
who lives in the United States and is quite

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:01.500
extreme.

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:05.680
He’s part of what is known as the Alt-right,
and well, his approaches are a cocktail loaded

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:12.600
with anti-communism, anti-globalism, anti-cultural
Marxism, anti-LGBT and a lot of conservatism.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:17.540
This character has even criticised the pragmatic
leaders in Bolsonaro's government.

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:23.530
Olavo de Carvalho qualifies Mourão (vice
president) as an idiot and says the government

00:10:23.530 --> 00:10:24.570
is running badly.)

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:28.530
Yes, that's right, there are already clashes
between the groups that make up the government.

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:32.530
And... if that’s within the government,
you can imagine how hard it’ll be to find

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:34.910
agreement with the rest of the parliamentary
forces.

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:39.000
In fact, in just three months the government
has already been defeated several times in

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:40.000
Congress.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:47.520
But, folks, we still need to talk about Bolsonaro’s
third handicap.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:52.480
(THE ECONOMY DOESN’T WORK)

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:57.500
We should state that this isn’t Bolsonaro’s
fault as it stems fundamentally from the disaster

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:01.900
caused by the previous government, the Workers'
Party, which led Brazil into one of its worst

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:03.480
crises in 2016.

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:09.720
But even if it’s not their fault, Bolsonaro’s
government is going to have to deal with it

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:11.810
and work with an economy that’s not performing.

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:24.390
Basically, the country has a huge public deficit
of between 7 and 8% per year.

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:28.430
Public debt keeps growing and is already at
around 80% - which is a lot for a country

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:29.430
like Brazil.

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:34.340
To make matters worse, there are 13 million
unemployed and the economy is struggling.

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:39.320
(Citi cuts 2019 Brazil GDP growth forecast
to 1.8 percent.

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:40.320
Reuters)

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:44.010
Faced with this situation, Brazil almost doesn’t
need a Minister of Economy, they need Doctor

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:45.010
House.

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:48.170
Obviously such a big deficit doesn’t give
the government much margin for anything and

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:51.850
if you have such huge imbalances and no growth,
things will only get harder.

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:54.790
There’s only one solution: budget cuts.

00:11:54.790 --> 00:12:01.080
And that’s precisely what the government
wants to do with pensions, which is by far

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:03.440
the largest expenditure for the Brazilian
government.

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:07.780
More than 40% of the government's budget goes
to pensions… and some estimates even suggest

00:12:07.780 --> 00:12:12.990
that in the next decade this rise to above
60%.

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:16.700
This is not surprising because Brazil has
one of the most generous systems in the world.

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:20.940
The country’s average retirement age is
54 years and the average pension is equivalent

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:23.370
to 70% of the final salary.

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:27.820
That means, you can retire at 54 and for the
rest of your life you get 70% of the salary

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:30.240
you were earning just before you retired.

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:32.630
Obviously, this is not sustainable.

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:35.880
"Brazil has one of the most generous systems
in the world.

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:40.810
If there is no pension reform, ... Brazil
will head towards insolvency and a debt crisis."

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:45.530
Chris Garman, managing director of Eurasia
Group, a political risk advisory firm.)

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:46.610
The problem?

00:12:46.610 --> 00:12:47.740
Cuts are never popular…

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:51.210
And folks, if Bolsonaro really wants to change
Brazil for good, he’s going to have to negotiate,

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:54.390
and that means a lot of compromise.

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:55.860
The question is, can he do it?

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:58.950
Leave your answer in the comments and in this
survey.

00:12:58.950 --> 00:13:02.960
Anyway, as you can see, these are the factors
that have overshadowed Bolsonaro’s first

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:04.290
months as President of Brazil.

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:08.510
If he manages to build a coalition of political
support to transform the Brazilian economy,

00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:10.300
we’ll be glad to tell you all about it.

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:13.210
And if he doesn’t, his government may not
last very long.

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:17.060
So I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did, and don’t forget to

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:18.490
subscribe for brand new videos.

